<!--
  THE PALETTE (PLAN.md §6 "Colour is a token, never a literal", §11 M16).

  Every chrome colour in the application comes from a key in this file. A hard-coded brush anywhere
  in src/ is a bug, because it survives the theme swap: that is exactly how the toolbar, the status
  bar and the area around the page stayed mid-grey with High Contrast on until M16, putting a black
  button on #6B6B6B at 3.96:1 against §6's 7:1 requirement.

  Tokens are named for the ROLE they play, never for the colour they hold. The accent is navy on a
  light ground and a pale blue on a dark one — navy is 1.68:1 on black and structurally unusable
  there — and no key called "Navy" could have done that.

  DERIVED FROM THE APPLICATION ICON, NOT ADOPTED FROM IT. assets-src/icons/README.md states three
  brand colours: navy #1E335C, gold #C8A24B, rule grey #9AA5B8. Two of the three cannot be used as
  written. The gold is 2.41:1 on white and the rule grey 2.49:1 — neither can carry text, and the
  grey cannot carry a boundary that has to be perceived (WCAG 1.4.11 asks 3:1). So:
    * the gold became Ornament.OnAccent, THE KEY NAME STATING ITS ONLY LEGAL BACKGROUND;
    * the rule grey was walked darker to #6E7A8F wherever a boundary must be seen, and the original
      survives only as Chrome.Divider, which is decorative and declared as such below.
  "Masonic blue" works as a chrome palette. "Masonic blue AND GOLD" on white chrome does not.

  CHROME.MUTED COLLAPSES TO FULL WHITE IN HIGH CONTRAST. There is no de-emphasised text at 7:1;
  size and weight carry the hierarchy instead. Written down because a reviewer will otherwise
  helpfully "fix" it back to a grey.

  FOCUS IS TWO TOKENS, not one. A single ring colour cannot clear 3:1 against both a near-white
  chrome background and the navy accent fill at once — 3:1 on near-white needs a relative luminance
  below about 0.17, 3:1 on navy needs one above about 0.20. So the ring is drawn the way Fluent's
  own is: Focus outside, Focus.Inner inside, each contrasting with the other. Whatever the ring
  lands on, one of the two is visible against it.

  THE PAGE NEVER THEMES. Page.Sheet is #FFFFFF in all three variants: the user is laying out
  something that will be printed on paper (docs/M9-spec.md §4). It is declared here — though no
  control paints with it — so that Page.Backdrop has something its floor can be measured against.

  THE TWO ADORNMENT TOKENS ARE THE SAME IN LIGHT AND DARK, and that is not an oversight. They are
  painted ON THE SHEET (M17: the empty-frame hint and the hover outline), and the sheet is white in
  every variant, so a Dark value would be a pale grey on white paper — unreadable, and wrong for the
  same reason Page.Sheet does not theme. High Contrast still moves, because there the floor moves.

  ONE ELEVATION WAS NOT ENOUGH, AND THE SECOND ONE IS ARGUED RATHER THAN ASSUMED (§11 M76).
  Until M76 the menu bar, the toolbar, the action panel, the status bar and every dialog body all
  painted Chrome.Background, and buttons painted whatever Fluent handed them — a window with no
  figure and no ground, in which the M37 borders did the entire structural job alone.
  Chrome.Surface is the raised plane those things now sit on: button fills, list and form cards,
  the page rail's tiles. Chrome.Background is untouched — every value, every pair already
  measured against it — because darkening the ground instead would have re-measured fourteen
  pairs to achieve the identical visual step.

  THE SURFACE/GROUND PAIR IS 1.15:1 AND IS DECLARED decorative. A reviewer should challenge that,
  so here is the answer. It is legitimate for exactly one reason: THE TONE IS NEVER THE BOUNDARY.
  A button is bounded by its M37 border, a card by the Rule on the side that meets the ground, the
  panel by the rule it already carries. The tonal step is reinforcement and comfort, nothing more,
  so the rule that colour is never the ONLY signal is not merely satisfied here — colour is not
  ANY of the signal, only the softening of one that is already drawn. That is the same declaration
  Chrome.Divider carries and for the same reason: a difference nobody has to perceive must never
  be quietly promoted into one somebody does.

  IN HIGH CONTRAST THE TWO COLLAPSE TO THE SAME BLACK, ON PURPOSE. Nothing is lost, because there
  Border.Thickness steps to 2px and does the whole job by itself — which is exactly the point of
  the paragraph above. Written down because this, too, will otherwise be helpfully "fixed" into a
  near-black that would give a high-contrast user a smudge where they had an edge.

  ==== CONTRAST ====
  ThemeCompositionTests parses the block below OUT OF THIS FILE, recomputes every ratio from the
  hex values above it, and fails if a number here is wrong or a floor is missed. The ratios are not
  documentation that can rot; they are the test's expected values. Floors are WCAG: 4.5 for text,
  3 for a boundary that must be perceived. IN HIGH CONTRAST EVERY FLOOR IS RAISED TO 7 (§6),
  except for pairs declared `decorative`, which have no floor anywhere.

  PAIR Chrome.Foreground on Chrome.Background >= 4.5 : Light 15.45  Dark 14.98  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Chrome.Muted on Chrome.Background >= 4.5 : Light 6.54  Dark 7.72  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Chrome.Border on Chrome.Background >= 3 : Light 3.76  Dark 5.39  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Chrome.Divider on Chrome.Background >= decorative : Light 2.16  Dark 1.61  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Accent on Chrome.Background >= 3 : Light 10.83  Dark 7.74  HighContrast 16.75
  PAIR Accent.Foreground on Accent >= 4.5 : Light 12.48  Dark 8.31  HighContrast 16.75
  PAIR Ornament.OnAccent on Accent >= 3 : Light 5.18  Dark 5.36  HighContrast 16.75
  PAIR Page.Backdrop on Page.Sheet >= 3 : Light 4.34  Dark 13.58  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Focus on Chrome.Background >= 3 : Light 15.45  Dark 14.98  HighContrast 19.56
  PAIR Focus.Inner on Focus >= 3 : Light 17.79  Dark 16.08  HighContrast 19.56
  PAIR Focus.Inner on Accent >= 3 : Light 12.48  Dark 8.31  HighContrast 16.75
  PAIR Warning on Chrome.Background >= 4.5 : Light 6.81  Dark 8.35  HighContrast 10.64
  PAIR Adornment.Hint on Page.Sheet >= 4.5 : Light 5.33  Dark 5.33  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Adornment.Hover on Page.Sheet >= 3 : Light 4.91  Dark 4.91  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Chrome.Foreground on Chrome.Surface >= 4.5 : Light 17.79  Dark 13.05  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Chrome.Muted on Chrome.Surface >= 4.5 : Light 7.54  Dark 6.73  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Chrome.Border on Chrome.Surface >= 3 : Light 4.34  Dark 4.70  HighContrast 21.00
  PAIR Accent on Chrome.Surface >= 3 : Light 12.48  Dark 6.75  HighContrast 16.75
  PAIR Focus on Chrome.Surface >= 3 : Light 17.79  Dark 13.05  HighContrast 19.56
  PAIR Warning on Chrome.Surface >= 4.5 : Light 7.85  Dark 7.28  HighContrast 10.64
  PAIR Chrome.Surface on Chrome.Background >= decorative : Light 1.15  Dark 1.15  HighContrast 1.00
  ==== END CONTRAST ====

  Two rules bind absolutely and both are tested: colour is never the only signal (Border.Thickness
  and Focus.Thickness step up in High Contrast, so every rule that changes a colour also changes a
  border), and an icon never appears without its label.
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:theme="clr-namespace:TrestleBoard.App.Theme">
  <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

    <!-- ============================ LIGHT ============================ -->
    <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Background" Color="#EDEFF2" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Surface" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Foreground" Color="#14181F" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Muted" Color="#4A5567" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Border" Color="#6E7A8F" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Divider" Color="#9AA5B8" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Accent" Color="#1E335C" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Accent.Foreground" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Ornament.OnAccent" Color="#C8A24B" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Page.Backdrop" Color="#6E7A8F" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Page.Sheet" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Adornment.Hint" Color="#6B6B6B" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Adornment.Hover" Color="#2A6FCF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Focus" Color="#14181F" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Focus.Inner" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Warning" Color="#A31515" />
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Border.Thickness">1</Thickness>
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Focus.Thickness">2</Thickness>
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Rule.Top">0,1,0,0</Thickness>
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Rule.Bottom">0,0,0,1</Thickness>
      <!-- SHAPE IS A TOKEN TOO (§11 M76). A corner radius typed into forty control themes is a
           forty-place decision; here it is one. Control is what a button or a field rounds by,
           Surface what a card, a tile or a panel rounds by — the larger plane takes the larger
           radius so the two read as different kinds of thing rather than as one sloppy one. -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="TrestleBoard.Radius.Control">6</CornerRadius>
      <CornerRadius x:Key="TrestleBoard.Radius.Surface">10</CornerRadius>
      <!-- Spent on the page sheet and on nothing else: the sheet is the one object in the window
           pretending to be a physical thing, and a shadow is how paper says so. -->
      <BoxShadows x:Key="TrestleBoard.Elevation.Sheet">0 2 12 0 #33000000</BoxShadows>
    </ResourceDictionary>

    <!-- ============================ DARK ============================= -->
    <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Dark">
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Background" Color="#1C1F26" />
      <!-- One step ABOVE the ground, never below it: Chrome.Background keeps every value and
           every measured pair it had, and the raised plane is the new token. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Surface" Color="#262A33" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Foreground" Color="#F2F4F8" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Muted" Color="#A8B2C4" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Border" Color="#8A94A6" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Divider" Color="#3A4150" />
      <!-- Navy is 1.68:1 on this background. The accent has to become light when the ground does. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Accent" Color="#8FB4E8" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Accent.Foreground" Color="#10182A" />
      <!-- Gold on a PALE accent is 1.6:1, so the ornament darkens with its background. The key
           name promised only that it works on the accent, and here that means a deep bronze. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Ornament.OnAccent" Color="#4A3708" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Page.Backdrop" Color="#2A2E38" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Page.Sheet" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Adornment.Hint" Color="#6B6B6B" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Adornment.Hover" Color="#2A6FCF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Focus" Color="#F2F4F8" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Focus.Inner" Color="#10182A" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Warning" Color="#FF9E9E" />
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Border.Thickness">1</Thickness>
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Focus.Thickness">2</Thickness>
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Rule.Top">0,1,0,0</Thickness>
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Rule.Bottom">0,0,0,1</Thickness>
      <!-- Identical to Light: shape does not theme. See the Light dictionary. -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="TrestleBoard.Radius.Control">6</CornerRadius>
      <CornerRadius x:Key="TrestleBoard.Radius.Surface">10</CornerRadius>
      <!-- Deeper than Light for the same apparent softness: black bleeds into a dark backdrop
           faster than it does into a pale one. -->
      <BoxShadows x:Key="TrestleBoard.Elevation.Sheet">0 2 12 0 #59000000</BoxShadows>
    </ResourceDictionary>

    <!-- ========================= HIGH CONTRAST ======================= -->
    <!-- Inherits Dark (Theme/AppTheme.cs), so every FluentTheme control resource this file does not
         name resolves to its Dark value instead of going missing. The accents are the overlay's
         own, which had already checked them against black: cyan 16.75:1, yellow 19.56:1,
         red 10.64:1. Focus and selection are yellow because they must be unmistakable at a glance,
         and because yellow on black is the highest-contrast pairing available after plain white. -->
    <ResourceDictionary x:Key="{x:Static theme:AppTheme.HighContrast}">
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Background" Color="#000000" />
      <!-- Deliberately the same black as the ground: see the header. The border steps to 2px
           here and carries the whole boundary on its own, so the tonal step has nothing left
           to reinforce and a near-black-on-black step would only muddy it. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Surface" Color="#000000" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Foreground" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <!-- Deliberately white: there is no de-emphasised text at 7:1. See the header. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Muted" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Border" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Chrome.Divider" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Accent" Color="#00FFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Accent.Foreground" Color="#000000" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Ornament.OnAccent" Color="#000000" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Page.Backdrop" Color="#000000" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Page.Sheet" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Adornment.Hint" Color="#000000" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Adornment.Hover" Color="#000000" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Focus" Color="#FFFF00" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Focus.Inner" Color="#000000" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TrestleBoard.Warning" Color="#FF9E9E" />
      <!-- The non-colour half of "colour is never the only signal". -->
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Border.Thickness">2</Thickness>
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Focus.Thickness">3</Thickness>
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Rule.Top">0,2,0,0</Thickness>
      <Thickness x:Key="TrestleBoard.Rule.Bottom">0,0,0,2</Thickness>
      <!-- Identical to Light and Dark, and it does NOT collapse here the way the tonal step does:
           a radius is a shape, and shape is the signal High Contrast leans ON rather than the one
           it gives up. Rounding a control never costs a boundary — the 2px border still draws it. -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="TrestleBoard.Radius.Control">6</CornerRadius>
      <CornerRadius x:Key="TrestleBoard.Radius.Surface">10</CornerRadius>
      <!-- None. White sheet on black backdrop is already 21:1, and a blur under a high-contrast
           user's page is noise added to the one thing they most need crisp. -->
      <BoxShadows x:Key="TrestleBoard.Elevation.Sheet">none</BoxShadows>
    </ResourceDictionary>

  </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>
</ResourceDictionary>
